  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWater vapor/RAP analysis has a 120+ knot H25 jet stretching from the \nUpper Mississippi Valley area north and east across the Great Lakes \nand into Quebec while a robust mid/upper ridge was situated over the \nTennessee Valley area. This puts the Central Plains region on the \nsouthern periphery of the more robust mid/upper SW flow. A moist \nplume evident on moisture channel imagery was situated across the \narea with PWATs around 2 inches. A cold front was bisecting Kansas \nlate this morning stretching from SW Kansas north and east into \nnortheast Kansas. This front is progged to accelerate southward this \nafternoon providing a focus for showers and thunderstorms. The \nairmass along and south of the front is expected to have large \ndewpoint depressions with steep low-level lapse rates and moderate \nCAPE which will be favorable for strong/severe wind gusts with the \nstrongest storm activity. Some storm activity will linger into the \nevening hours and perhaps during overnight hours as a weak LLJ noses \ninto south central and southeast KS. Some training of storms along \nand behind the front could lead to locally heavy rain.\n\nRising heights are progged across the area on Wed as the frontal \nboundary mixes northward resulting in another hot day across the \nregion. Any lingering morning shower activity should be confined to \nparts of central KS early in the period. Afternoon highs are \nexpected to rising into the upper 90s once again. Normal highs for \nmid Sep are in the low to mid 80s.\n\nThu-Sat...the mid/upper ridge will continue to impact the area \nthrough the period with dry conditions anticipated Thu-Fri. The heat \nwill persist into Saturday with most areas seeing highs in the \nupper 90s, but a shortwave trough moving over the Northern Rockies \non Sat may help break down this ridge and bring increasing chances \nfor showers and storms to the area Sat night into Sunday. Another \nimpactful cold front is also expected to arrive on Sunday ushering \nin more seasonable air through early next week. Some shower activity \nmay linger into early next week but confidence in the larger-scale \ndetails remains rather low at this time.",
